## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only merging with cause-based triage. This proposal supersedes
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) when the desk accepts it;
the accepted historical record remains unchanged. The old triage page is stale,
and its subject-only rule contradicts the behavior of its own interactive examples.

The claim on [incident #4471](/kb/incidents/4471) that one fix covers the ticket is
not supported by the rendered evidence. Split its parser and hint problems into
separate tickets. A known status records awareness, not resolution.

## What the export actually shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The **parser** reads month first for tenants whose locale writes day first. For
example, report R-41200 says a reminder for 7 April went out on 4 July. Fix the
shared parser to use the tenant's date convention and reject ambiguous input
when the convention is unavailable.

The **hint** problem occurs through booking-v3 forms for day-first tenants.
The field prints MM/DD/YYYY while the form interprets the entry using the tenant's
day-first convention. Align the displayed hint with the form's actual parser.
For example, R-41230 reports 6 January appearing as 1 June for copper.studio.
Changing only the shared parser cannot repair this contradictory instruction.

Use tenant configuration and channel, not the reporter's browser locale or
intake tag. The latter records an intake guess, not a confirmed cause.

<ReviewEvidence />

## The queue still needs attention

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April onward; merging began in July. The July merge spike
is backlog processing, not the start of the incident. New reports continue into
October, after the incident page's last review. The export ends on 4 October;
it cannot establish what happened after that snapshot or prove a fix shipped.

## How the desk should triage now

1. Deduplicate export rows by report ID, then select the ticket being investigated.
2. Read the body and look up the tenant's locale and booking-form version.
3. Match a supported cause using that configuration and the report's channel.
4. Merge only when the cause matches. A shared subject is insufficient; different
   subjects can still describe the same cause.
5. Keep unknown tenants and contradictory symptoms for investigation. Do not
   force them into a known cause merely because their subject looks familiar.
6. Track incoming and merged timestamps separately; review continuing reports
   and regression evidence before closing either ticket.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Verification and follow-up

Test the shared parser with day-first tenants across app and email channels,
booking-v2 forms, ambiguous dates and days above twelve. Test booking-v3 hints
against the tenant-aware form parser independently. Include a month-first
tenant as a control and confirm that a fix does not reverse valid dates.

The supplied classifier is a routing rule, not proof of every report's root
cause. Investigate the exceptional bodies separately before asserting a third
confirmed bug. Keep their existing count assignment visible until evidence
supports moving them.

